{{also|revolvé}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|revolven||to change direction}}, borrowed from {{der|en|fro|revolver||to reflect upon}}, from {{der|en|la|revolvō|revolvere|t=turn over, roll back, reflect upon}}, from {{m|la|re-||back}} + {{m|la|volvō||roll}}; see {{m|en|voluble}}, {{m|en|volve}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈvɒlv/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-revolve.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɒlv|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{i|Physical movement.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|now|rare}} To [[bring back]] {{m|en|into}} a particular place or condition; to [[restore]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause (something) to turn around a central point. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
## {{senseid|en|orbit}}{{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[orbit]] a central point (especially of a celestial body). {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|The Earth '''revolves''' around the sun.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To rotate around an [[axis]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|The Earth '''revolves''' once every twenty-four hours.}}
##* {{RQ:Birmingham Gossamer|chapter=I|passage=It is never possible to settle down to the ordinary routine of life at sea until the screw begins to '''revolve'''. There is an hour or two, after the passengers have embarked, which is disquieting and fussy.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[move]] in order or sequence. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
##: {{ux|en|The program '''revolves''' through all the queues before returning to the start.}}
# {{i|Mental activity.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|now|rare}} To [[ponder]] on; to [[reflect]] repeatedly upon; to [[consider]] all aspects of. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Richardson Clarissa|passage=These are the difficulties which arise to me on '''revolving''' this scheme […].|letter=82}}
##* {{RQ:Carlyle Past and Present|book=2|chapter=chapter 6, Monk Samson|text=He sits silent, '''revolving''' many thoughts, at the foot of St. Edmund’s Shrine.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[read]] through, to [[study]] (a book, author etc.). {{defdate|15th–19th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Regained|text=This having heard, strait I again '''revolv’d''' / The Law and Prophets.}}

